Here’s a practical RabbitMQ vs Apache Kafka comparison for Java / Spring Boot / microservices interviews
(OTP, SMS, OBD jobs, event pipelines, etc.).

Core mental model:
- RabbitMQ = smart broker + queues (route work, ack, retry/DLQ)
- Kafka = durable distributed log (store events, replay, many independent consumers)


🆚 RabbitMQ vs Apache Kafka — Full Comparison
| Feature                      | **RabbitMQ**                                              | **Apache Kafka**                                              |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Type**                     | Traditional message broker                                | Distributed event streaming platform                          |
| **Storage model**            | Queue-based (message removed after ack*)                  | Append-only log (retained by time/size policy)                |
| **Message model**            | Mostly broker push to consumers                           | Consumers pull / fetch                                        |
| **Routing**                  | Excellent (Exchange + routing key + binding)              | Topic + key → partition (weaker “smart routing”)              |
| **Use case**                 | Task/work queues, async jobs, complex routing             | Event sourcing, pipelines, analytics, audit streams           |
| **Message storage**          | Memory and/or disk (durable queue + persistent msg)       | Disk by design (partition log on brokers)                     |
| **Retention**                | Usually until consumed/acked (or TTL/DLQ)                 | Keep for hours/days/longer even after consume                 |
| **Replay**                   | Not natural                                               | Easy (rewind offsets)                                         |
| **Throughput**               | High for many apps, usually lower than Kafka at extreme scale | Very high (designed for massive streams)                   |
| **Delivery**                 | At-most / at-least (exactly-once not the common strength) | At-least-once common; EOS possible but complex                |
| **Consumer model**           | Competing consumers on one queue; pub/sub via multi-queue | Consumer groups (share partitions); different groups = fan-out |
| **Ordering**                 | Per queue                                                 | Per partition                                                 |
| **Protocol**                 | AMQP (+ MQTT/STOMP/etc.)                                  | Kafka protocol over TCP                                       |
| **Retry / DLQ**              | Native / easy                                             | Usually custom (retry topics / DLQ topics)                    |
| **Setup**                    | Usually simpler for job queues                            | More moving parts (brokers, partitions, offsets, rebalance)   |
| **Spring Boot**              | `spring-boot-starter-amqp`                                | `spring-kafka`                                                |
| **Best for**                 | OTP, SMS, OBD/job workers, workflow routing               | Transaction events, clickstream, logs, analytics              |

*With persistence configured, RabbitMQ can keep unacked messages on disk across restarts.
Do NOT say “RabbitMQ is only in-memory.”


🧩 When to Use What?

✅ Use RabbitMQ when:
- You need task queues (OTP, SMS, OBD call jobs)
- You need flexible routing (direct/topic/fanout/headers)
- You want easy retries + Dead Letter Queues
- Work is “do this job now”, not “keep this event forever for replay”

💡 OBD/SMS style:
RabbitMQ is often ideal to distribute call/SMS jobs to workers with ack/retry/DLQ.

✅ Use Kafka when:
- High-volume event streams (telemetry, logs, payments events)
- You need persistence + replay / event sourcing
- Many independent services must read the same stream (different groupIds)
- Downstream analytics / search / lake ingestion

💡 After SMS/call interaction events (clicked link, survey completed):
Kafka is great to stream those events into analytics/DB/Elasticsearch.


🔑 Biggest interview differences (memorize these)
1) RabbitMQ removes (or finishes) work from queue after successful ack.
   Kafka keeps events in the log for retention window.
2) RabbitMQ routing is smarter (exchanges/bindings).
   Kafka scales by partitions + consumer groups.
3) Same Kafka topic + different groups = every service gets full stream (fan-out).
   RabbitMQ fan-out = multiple queues bound to fanout/topic exchange.
4) Kafka replay = change offsets. RabbitMQ replay = usually not natural.


📦 Spring Boot Integration (quick)

🐇 RabbitMQ
```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-amqp</artifactId>
</dependency>
```
- Producer: `RabbitTemplate.convertAndSend(exchange, routingKey, message)`
- Consumer: `@RabbitListener(queues = "...")`

🐘 Kafka
```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.kafka</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-kafka</artifactId>
</dependency>
```
- Producer: `KafkaTemplate.send(topic, key, message)`
- Consumer: `@KafkaListener(topics = "...", groupId = "...")`

