🎯 1-liner Summary
- RabbitMQ = reliable broker for jobs + flexible routing + retries/DLQ
- Kafka = high-throughput durable event log for streaming, fan-out, and replay


🎤 Interview-style Answers

Q: Main differences between RabbitMQ and Kafka?
A: RabbitMQ is a message broker for reliable task/work delivery with rich routing via exchanges.
It is typically push-based and great for short-lived jobs (OTP/SMS/OBD).
Kafka is a distributed event log: pull-based, partition-scaled, retention-based, excellent for replay,
high throughput, and many independent consumer groups reading the same stream.

Q: When choose RabbitMQ over Kafka?
A: When I need flexible routing, DLQ/retries, and moderate async job processing
(OTP, notifications, outbound dialing workers), and I don’t need long retention/replay.

Q: When choose Kafka over RabbitMQ?
A: When I need durable event history, replay, very high throughput, or many services consuming
the same events independently (fraud + notify + ledger style).

Q: Can both be used in one system?
A: Yes. Common pattern: RabbitMQ for command/job execution; Kafka for domain event streaming/audit/analytics.


